## Slide 4 — Business Problem

**Time: 1 minute 15 seconds**

Today, GenAI validation in many enterprise environments is still handled through manual prompt testing, SME review, security review, and a limited set of sample scenarios.

That may be enough for a prototype, but it is not enough for production.

Once an AI assistant is live, users do not behave like demo users. They ask edge-case questions. They ask for sensitive information. They try to override instructions. They may ask the assistant to approve something outside its authority. And sometimes the model gives an answer that sounds confident, but is not grounded in approved enterprise documents.

That creates four major risks: **hallucination, PII leakage, jailbreak or role override, and policy-boundary violations**.

This directly affects **AI deployment teams, compliance leaders, security teams, CISOs, and business owners**. All of them need evidence before a GenAI solution goes live.

In BFSI especially, this is critical because governance, privacy, auditability, and trust are not optional. A production release cannot depend only on “it worked in the demo.”

It needs a repeatable pre-production safety gate that gives a clear decision: **block or approve**.

